From 7cd30601435ac198ecef7dae8c0b47162924ea27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stew675 <stew675@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 07:27:24 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] wip: the transport buffer COUNT comes from the arena
 RESIDENCY, not from free VRAM at startup

Now that the ping-pong buffers are a carve INSIDE the slab (released at every prefill -> decode transition), the
count is no longer a VRAM question at scheduler construction -- it is the trade the maintainer named: three
buffers are ~2 % faster at prefill than two, but the third one's set (~1.1 GiB on the field GSQ shape) comes out
of the DECODE arena, and on a shape whose arena cannot hold a good working/hot set that costs more decode than
the prefill gains.

The decision moves to the point where the arena is settled -- the "MoE expert cache (auto): arena X MiB of Y host
experts (Z% residency)" line, where the slab has grown -- and uses the maintainer's rule, with one buffer of slack
at each step because the marginal question is what the NEXT buffer leaves behind:

  * arena left after TWO buffers   < 20 % of the host set  ->  1 buffer
  * arena left after THREE buffers < 40 % of the host set  ->  2 buffers
  * otherwise                                              ->  3 buffers

The old startup decision is GONE, not shadowed: llama_context no longer multiplies anything by a depth.  It
publishes the one thing only it knows -- ONE BUFFER's bytes (the largest host-weight split set, floored by the
largest table, + 512 B) and the VRAM/4 cap -- through a new slab_set_ring_unit hook, and the backend DERIVES the
request as "unit x depth", capped, in one place.  The depth has exactly one owner:
ggml_backend_cuda_transport_bufs() (declared in ggml-cuda.h), driven by the residency rule and frozen after its
first decision (a count that follows every arena re-size flaps, and a 1-buffer graph costs ~21 %).

The generic layers keep no copy: the scheduler's ping-pong depth and the Meta backend's per-device ring depth both
read it through the EXISTING generic slab_ring_slots device hook, so they cannot drift from the region the slab
carved (they disagreed once already: 8 slots vs a 3-buffer region, and every staged input was refused).  The
scheduler re-derives its depth per graph execution (the decision lands after it was constructed) and only falls
back to the free-VRAM rule for a transport that is NOT slab-backed.  A changed request also drops an IDLE carve,
so the first prefill of a process uses the decided depth instead of the provisional one (guarded on wide_armed: a
live wide view sits above the region).

Field GSQ server, three requests (645 / 71 816 / 239 tok), same build, 3 buffers (the control) vs the rule's 2:

  decision      transport buffers: 3 -> 2 (arena 14 707.4 MiB = 35.9 % of 40 925.0 MiB; after 2 buffers 30.4 %,
                after 3 27.6 %; thresholds 20 / 40) -> region request 3412.5 -> 2275.0 MiB, carve 3456 -> 2304
  prefill       arena during the 71 816-token prefill 9199.8/9057.4/8915.1/8788.3 -> 10411.8/10305.7/10199.6/
                10057.2 MiB resident (+1.2 GiB back to the hot set); prefill 2209.4 -> 2224.6 t/s (noise)
  decode arena  16 049.9 -> 15 879.7 -> 15 576.0  becomes  15 960.2 -> 15 960.2 -> 15 827.7 MiB

Campaign arm unchanged: 1 GPU 89k, 3 cycles, text 43d0b1f4629e on every run, prefill 2483.8 t/s (2493.7 before,
spread 4.8 %).
